# panel-exceptions.psv -- programs whose panel is RIGHT as it stands, though
# tools/audit_panels.py would score it as failing.  name | why
#
# An exception is a debt with a reason attached.  Every line here should be
# one that a stranger reading the card would agree with; the moment a
# better card is possible, the line comes out.
perr|perr prints the text of an OS-9 error number; error text IS its output
elvprsv|run by elvis on a crash to preserve the buffer; by hand with nothing preserved it prints nothing, and the card says so
exrecover|recovers a buffer expreserve kept when vi died; with nothing preserved "File not found" is its whole answer, and the card says so
hist|a line editor in front of the shell; on this console it prints a row of asterisks and returns, and the card says so
if|its help and a try: it hands the chosen branch to Microware's shell, which is not on this disk, so the branch never runs; the card says so
qp|its help and a try: it runs the expanded line through Microware's shell, which is not on this disk; the card says so
checkenv|prints its syntax line on every call and answers only in its status; the card shows both statuses
run|its usage and a try; the program it runs answers on the terminal PORT names, and on this console nothing comes back
su|GNU su's usage and a try to become tester, which on a disk owned 0.0 throughout has no one to become
epson|writes its control codes to the printer itself; with no printer on the disk its option list is what there is to show, and the card says so
lmargin|writes its control codes to the printer itself; with no printer on the disk its option list is what there is to show, and the card says so
splman|Carl Kreider's spooler manager wants a printer on a serial device; without one it prints nothing and returns, and the card says so
splprt|the spooler's printer process wants a printer device; without one it prints nothing and returns, and the card says so
lp|there is no spooler running on this disk; its option list is what there is to show, and the card says so
lprm|there is no spooler running on this disk; its option list is what there is to show, and the card says so
lpsched|there is no printer device on this disk to start the spooler on; its option list is what there is to show
submit|a batch-file runner; its option list is what a card can show without a .sub job to run
chardef|loads a VT220 character set from a definition file, and no terminal here to load; its syntax is what there is to show
macunpack|opens PackIt archives and none is on the disk; its options are what there is to show
runc|the Lua runtime engine; a `luac -x' chunk runs through it by name at an OS-9 shell (like runb for BASIC09), so on its own it has no chunk to interpret
pacman|a keypad-controlled ASCII maze game (8/2/4/6 to move); it draws its maze in raw keyboard mode, which the capture harness does not drive, so there is no static frame to show
disktest|measures the disk and, run on one, ends the session; its syntax is what there is to show, and the card says so
trap|an example trap handler that cannot be installed from user state; the refusal is the whole of what it does here, and the card says so
convert|starts WORLD, whose opening shows only its MORE prompt here; the card says so
puzzle|a G-Windows program: at a terminal one line of its tile frame is all it draws, and the card says so
vtxtcn|writes its tables silently; the ls that follows on its card is the evidence
unstr|asks for 75 megabytes before it reads a byte and is refused; the stop is the whole of what it does, and the card says so
modbuster|prints only the name of the module it split out; the card shows that
texidx|builds an index from a TeX run's entries; with none to read its usage is what there is to show
collect|a g++ linker-helper pass; with nothing to link its usage is what there is to show
gcc_collect|a g++ linker-helper pass; with nothing to link its usage is what there is to show
gpp_collect|a g++ linker-helper pass; with nothing to link its usage is what there is to show
cron|a scheduling daemon; its card shows the crontab it reads rather than running it
t|a stub that returns success and prints nothing; the status line on its card is the evidence
eset|the event directory is empty and nothing here creates an event, so it can only report that
eunlink|the event directory is empty, so it reports the event is not found
brushtopbm|reads a Xerox brush file, no sample ships; its card shows it naming what it wants
gouldtoppm|reads a Gould scanner file, no sample ships; its card shows it naming what it wants
hipstopgm|reads a HIPS image, no sample ships; its card shows it naming what it wants
hpcdtoppm|reads a PhotoCD image, no sample ships; its card shows it naming what it wants
mtvtoppm|reads an MTV/PRT ray-tracer image, no sample ships; its card shows it naming what it wants
spottopgm|reads a SPOT satellite image, no sample ships; its card shows its usage
ximtoppm|reads an Xim image, no sample ships; its card shows it naming what it wants
xvminitoppm|reads an XV thumbnail image, no sample ships; its card shows it naming what it wants
ptxminst|installs the ptxm path-table module into the kernel, which needs system state not available here; its usage is what there is to show
frm|lists who your mail is from; it does not recognize this disk's mail folder and reports no mail, where messages and readmsg read the same one
lmail|delivers mail locally; a real recipient makes it wait on a peer that never answers, so only its usage line is safe to show
mail|a simple mail sender; it opens a scratch file on /r0, the RAM disk this configuration has no device for, and stops there
mailx|the mail reader and sender; it finds a mailbox by user number, and the super user this runs as has none
rmail|delivers incoming mail, invoked by uuxqt not by hand; local delivery expects a mailbox directory where a plain file ships, so it stops
smail|the smart mail router; it needs a routing configuration and a peer to hand mail to, neither present, so its options are what there is to show
uucico|the UUCP transfer program; it dials or answers a UUCP peer over a serial line, of which there is none here, so its options are what there is to show
uuxqt|runs the commands a remote UUCP site queued here; it needs a queue and a peer to fill it, finds neither, and stops at once
uulog|shows the UUCP transfer log; no UUCP traffic has been logged on this disk, so there is nothing to print
finger|asks another machine who is logged in; it needs a network to reach, and prints its usage without one
msntp|sets the system clock from a network time server; with no network here it cannot open an NTP socket
blastem|XMODEM and YMODEM file transfer for the MM/1; it needs a modem on a serial path, and its help is what there is to show without one
byteflip|byte-swaps a dbz database between architectures; with no database to swap it ends the shell rather than printing anything
newslock|the news system's file lock; it belongs to a news spool this configuration does not run, and leaves no trace here
connect|joins two serial paths so you can talk through one to the other; there are no serial devices here, so its usage is what there is to show
disable|turns MTSMon off on a serial port; it needs the port's driver module resident to find it, and none is loaded
enable|the counterpart to disable on a serial port; it too needs the port's driver module resident, and none is loaded
cls|clears the screen through the terminal's termcap clear capability; a cleared screen is the whole of its output
fkeys|sets a VT220 terminal's function keys from a definition file; it needs VT220 hardware to program, and names its file argument without one
setfont|loads a downloadable font into a terminal from a font file; it needs terminal hardware to receive the font, and prints its usage without one
sbreak|sends a break signal on a serial device; there is no serial device here to send one on, so it reports that a device must be given
wysecrack|probes for a Wyse terminal by asking it to identify itself; nothing here is a Wyse, so no reply comes and the screen stays blank
uwho|lists who is logged in from the UNAXCESS bulletin board; it opens /etc/utmp, and a leading /etc names a device in OS-9, so it cannot find that file
authwn|WN's authenticator for a password-protected area; inetd and wn invoke it per request, and run alone it has nothing to authenticate
inetdc|what inetd forks to handle each connection; run by hand with nothing to serve it exits in silence
basicwin|an X11 basic-window demo; with no X server here it stops at "cannot connect to X server"
xengine|an X11 demo animating an engine; with no X display here it stops at "Can't open display"
loadmem|loads a file into memory at a hex address; it must run as the super user with addresses given, and prints its syntax without them
savemem|writes a block of memory out to a file; it must run as the super user with addresses given, and prints its syntax without them
snap|writes a snapshot of the screen on specific hardware terminals; it does not recognize this console, which is not one of them
getsys|reports OS-9's system globals; the emulator does not implement the system-global reads it makes, so only unimplemented notices come back
sysmax|reports the scheduler's maximum process age; the emulator keeps no such setting, so it reads back zero
signal|sends an OS-9 signal to a process named by id; with no target process its syntax is what there is to show
snd_sig|sends a signal to one or more processes named by id; with no target process its synopsis is what there is to show
transfer|copies files from GDOS disks to OS-9; it wants the GDOS device DGDOS0, which is not present
mvolformat|formats a set of floppies with sequential volume numbers; there is no floppy drive here, so its syntax is what there is to show
bootgen|builds an OS9Boot file from named modules for a machine to start from; there is no such file to build here, so it answers with its command format
bsplt68|takes an OS9Boot file apart into the modules inside it; there is no boot file here, so it answers with its usage line
dpark|restores an RBF disk's head to track zero; there is no disk head to park here, so it answers with its command format
setime|sets the system clock; it prompts for the date and time in YYMMDDHHMMSS order, and setting the clock is a privileged operation
rndir|renames subdirectory names to upper or lower case; on this case-insensitive filesystem a name and its other-case form are one entry, so there is nothing to change
lgrep|lists the files a pattern appears in; it prints nothing even for a pattern that is present, so grep -l does this job
fixyear|corrects a file's modification year when the clock wrote a wrong one; the files here carry good years, so it finds nothing to fix
os9lib|not a program but the RTF/68K FORTRAN runtime library; it is shown among the runtime modules, not run
edir|lists the OS-9 event directory; nothing on this disk creates an event, so the heading is all there is
pbmtobbnbg|writes graphics for a BBN BitGraph terminal; it rejects the PBM bitmaps this disk's netpbm makes with bad magic number and writes nothing
lpq|shows the print spooler's queue; no spooler is running on this disk, and it says so
lpshut|stops the print spooler; none is running here, and it says so
setterm|reports the terminal type in a single line -- too little for a published panel, but it does its one job
uuname|lists the UUCP site names, a few short lines below the publish threshold; it does its job
vis|redraws one command's output in place like watch; its control header is a single line, too little to publish
unpacklib.os9|the OS-9 build of unpacklib; extracts modules from a library until a sync mismatch, shown on the unpacklib card
ppmtopj|writes an HP PaintJet file silently; the pjtoppm round-trip that follows on its card, ending in "PPM raw, 48 by 24", is the evidence
ppmtorgb3|writes three RGB plane files silently; the ls that follows on its card, three 1165-byte files, is the evidence
rain|a screen toy; a still frame of falling raindrops is too sparse to score, and there is nothing denser to show
